1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,199
Future gave us one result with a timed
get. Real services often need a pipeline:
load something, transform it, combine it
with another call, handle failure, enforce
a timeout, then save.

2
00:00:11,319 --> 00:00:21,879
Blocking on each get in sequence works and
throws away concurrency. Nesting callbacks
by hand works and becomes unreadable. How
do we compose asynchronous work as a graph
of stages?

3
00:00:22,059 --> 00:00:33,140
CompletableFuture is Java's standard
answer for that composition. Look at this
java for a moment. supplyAsync starts load
on an async pool and yields a future of
its result.

4
00:00:33,259 --> 00:00:43,619
thenApply maps the value through transform
when load completes. orTimeout fails the
stage if the pipeline takes too long.
thenAccept consumes the final value in
save.

5
00:00:43,739 --> 00:00:57,019
The code reads top to bottom like a happy-
path story, while the machinery runs
asynchronously. thenApply versus
thenCompose is the composition subtlety
interviews love. thenApply maps a value to
another value.

6
00:00:57,139 --> 00:01:03,499
thenCompose maps a value to another future
and flattens the nesting — the async
cousin of flatMap.

7
00:01:03,619 --> 00:01:15,939
If transform itself returns a
CompletableFuture, thenApply gives you a
future of a future; thenCompose keeps one
layer: Look at this java for a moment.
Error handling belongs in the graph.

8
00:01:16,059 --> 00:01:27,699
exceptionally recovers a value from a
failure. handle sees success or failure
together. Swallowing exceptions inside a
stage without recovering or completing
exceptionally is how pipelines die
quietly.

9
00:01:27,819 --> 00:01:40,859
Preserve causes, decide policy, do not
erase failure. Executor choice matters.
supplyAsync without an executor uses the
common Fork/Join pool. That pool is
shared.

10
00:01:40,979 --> 00:01:53,379
Blocking on I/O inside common-pool stages
can starve other async work on the JVM —
including parallel streams. For blocking
I/O, pass an executor sized for that
workload: Look at this java for a moment.

11
00:01:53,499 --> 00:02:02,819
Timeouts and cancellation remain part of
the story. orTimeout and completeOnTimeout
define latency policy in the graph instead
of hoping callers remember to wrap get.

12
00:02:02,939 --> 00:02:13,939
Cancellation still cooperates with
interruptible work underneath. What if we
use thenApply where thenCompose was
needed, or block with get inside a common-
pool stage to "keep it simple"?

13
00:02:14,119 --> 00:02:26,839
Nested futures appear, or the common pool
stalls. Both bugs look like "async is
hard" when they are really operator
mismatch and pool misuse. thenCombine
earns a mention once pipelines grow
branches.

14
00:02:26,959 --> 00:02:36,959
You start two asynchronous loads, then
combine their results when both complete.
That is the graph thinking single futures
were missing: not only chains, but joins.

15
00:02:37,079 --> 00:02:42,759
Timeouts on the combined stage still
matter; a slow sibling should not pin a
request forever.

16
00:02:42,879 --> 00:02:49,240
Debugging a composed future means reading
the exception from the stage that failed
and remembering which executor ran which
lambda.

17
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:59,279
Silent exceptionally that returns null
converts a loud failure into a null that
explodes later — the same anti-pattern as
swallowing checked exceptions, wearing
async clothes.

18
00:02:59,399 --> 00:03:07,679
Picture loading a user profile and a set
of entitlements concurrently, then
combining them into a view model with a
one-second timeout on the join.

19
00:03:07,799 --> 00:03:18,999
thenCombine expresses the join; orTimeout
expresses the budget; exceptionally maps
failures to a safe fallback or an error
type. The graph matches the product
sentence.

20
00:03:19,119 --> 00:03:31,839
Keep stages small and pure when you can:
load, transform, save. Side effects in the
middle of a graph make cancellation and
retries harder. Composition is easiest
when each stage has one job.

21
00:03:31,959 --> 00:03:44,919
Hold the checklist: operators match shapes
(thenApply vs thenCompose); executors
match blocking; timeouts sit on the graph;
failures recover or surface with causes.

22
00:03:45,039 --> 00:03:56,199
Some workloads are not async I/O graphs —
they are CPU-heavy divide-and-conquer over
large arrays or trees. That shape has its
own pool and its own recursion style.
